PROPOSAL -- NOT APPLIED, NOT BUILT, NOT RUN.
reTS (game DLL and client DLL) change that makes TS's own cash the loadout rework's money, so the
equipment menu itself enforces the budget instead of the plugin refusing an apply after the fact.

Why it is a proposal. The rework needs none of this: the plugin reads the menu's commands, prices them
with the menu's own price table, refuses what does not fit and hands the weapons over, so with the
plugin alone a player can build a loadout the menu offers and the plugin then turns down (the menu's
money bar is a constant 30000), and the cash in his wallet is a display the plugin sets. This change
removes both oddities. It touches the game's economy for every server that loads the DLL, so it is gated
on the usecash cvar the match mode already sets (tsm_nocash 1 sets usecash 1), which is OFF in the game
as it ships.

What the source says today (all verified by reading, nothing run):
 - dlls/ts_buy.cpp: CashBased() is "return false" ("hard-OFF in the shipped game"); BuyWeapon,
   BuyAmmo and CTSGroundWeapon::GetWeapon carry their cost branches behind it, dead.
 - cl_dll/ts_buymenu.cpp: TS_BUDGET is the constant 30000 ("FUN_10036640 (CashBased off)"); the menu's
   affordability tests and its auto-ammo pass spend that, not the wallet.
 - The wallet is CBasePlayer::m_iCash, sent to the client as TSCash and drawn when usecash is on.

Proposed (the plugin's own pricing in ts_matchmode.sma stays as the backstop either way):

--- a/dlls/ts_buy.cpp
+++ b/dlls/ts_buy.cpp
@@ bool CashBased()
-// @ CashBased__Fv -- the shipped binary returns 0 unconditionally (cash charging disabled).
-bool CashBased()
-{
-	return false;
-}
+// @ CashBased__Fv -- the shipped binary returns 0 unconditionally (cash charging disabled); here it follows
+// the usecash cvar (m_iSrvUseCash in the rules, the cached cvar), which no server sets but a match mode.
+bool CashBased()
+{
+	return CVAR_GET_FLOAT("usecash") != 0.0f;
+}

@@ bool CBasePlayer::BuyWeapon(int type, int iAttach)
-	// Free in the shipped game (CashBased() == false). If a cash economy is ever turned
-	// on, this is where the price (get_weapon_info(type)->price, s_weapon_info+0xd8) would
-	// be checked against m_iCash and deducted.
+	// Priced when the economy is on: the weapon, its attachments (900 / 800 / 500 / 1800) -- refused
+	// when m_iCash does not cover it, charged once the pickup succeeds.
+	int price = 0;
+	if (CashBased())
+	{
+		const s_weapon_info *wi = get_weapon_info((tsweapon_t)type);
+		price = wi->iPrice;
+		if (iAttach & 1) price += 900;
+		if (iAttach & 2) price += 800;
+		if (iAttach & 4) price += 500;
+		if (iAttach & 8) price += 1800;
+		if (m_iCash < price)
+			return false;
+	}
 	// @0x846ca: the buy path selects the purchase AND records the buyer (the sole
 	// bRecordOwner caller -- item+0x30). iAttach = the tkw token's attach mask.
-	return PickUpWeapon(type, NULL, iAttach, true, true);
+	if (!PickUpWeapon(type, NULL, iAttach, true, true))
+		return false;
+	GiveCash(-price);
+	return true;

--- a/cl_dll/ts_buymenu.cpp
+++ b/cl_dll/ts_buymenu.cpp
@@
-static const int TS_BUDGET = 30000; // FUN_10036640 (CashBased off)
+// FUN_10036640: the menu's money. 30000 with the economy off (the game as it ships); the wallet when the
+// server runs one (SrvSett id 1, g_iTSUseCash), which is what the money bar and every "can I afford it"
+// test then measure.
+extern int g_iTSUseCash;                       // ts_status.cpp
+static int TsBudget()
+{
+	return g_iTSUseCash ? gHUD.m_TSStatus.m_iCash : 30000;
+}

and every use of TS_BUDGET in that file (lines 405, 442, 472, 584, 965, 1095, 1154, 1179, 1193, 1199,
1205, 1228, 1233, 1257 at the time of writing) becomes TsBudget(). m_iCash is private to CHudTSStatus
today; a one-line public accessor is needed.

What the plugin side does once this is built.
 - The plugin keeps pricing the apply (lr_cart_*), because a client is not trusted; with the DLL
   charging as well, a purchase is charged twice unless the plugin stops showing the remaining budget
   as the wallet and shows the allowance instead. Concretely: lr_wallet() would return the class's full
   allowance (lr_budget) rather than allowance minus the set's cost, and lr_regive() would not be the
   thing that gives the weapons -- the respawn the arm already does (task_arm_respawn / task_revive_arm
   -> spawn()) would apply the queue through BuyArmament, with weaponrestriction 0. That is a larger
   rework of the arm path than this proposal covers; the plugin-only version was chosen first for that
   reason.
 - The deny list and the 81-slot rule would move into the DLL's IsRestrictedWeapon mask.
 - kevlar: AddItem(0x10) -> TakeArmor(100) already works in the DLL (it is the reason the plugin's
   set_user_armor stand-in is a stand-in); with the DLL as the buyer the plugin stops granting it.

Risks if it is built: every non-H9 map with usecash 1 starts charging; a server that never ran cash
meets a menu whose money bar is its (empty) wallet; ts_wingiver victorycash / defeatcash keyvalues in
maps become real money. Gate the DLL change on tsm_enabled as ts_powerup.cpp does if that matters.
